import { asset } from "../lib/asset";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Eq from "../components/Eq.astro";
import R from "../data/results.json";
const small = R.grid.filter((g) => g.nk <= 20 && g.dataset !== "deep");
const lo = Math.min(...small.map((g) => g.vs_best)), hi = Math.max(...small.map((g) => g.vs_best));
const won = R.grid.filter((g) => g.vs_best < 0).length;
const sw = R.sweep[R.sweep.length - 1];
const deep = R.grid.find((g) => g.dataset === "deep");
const f1 = (x: number) => (x < 0 ? "−" : "+") + Math.abs(x).toFixed(1) + "%";
const f2 = (x: number) => (x < 0 ? "−" : "+") + Math.abs(x).toFixed(2) + "%";
const bibtex = `@techreport{monteiro2026hmeans,
  title  = {h-means: mini-batch k-means with a hierarchical warm start and Hartigan assignment},
  author = {Monteiro, H\\'elder},
  year   = {2026},
  note   = {Technical note. Code, logs and playground: https://github.com/heldernoid/hmeans}
}`;
---
<Base title="h-means" description="h-means: e-means with a hierarchical warm start and Hartigan's correction. Lower k-means loss at the same number of distance computations when clusters are small." page="index">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Technical note, November 2026</p>
    <h1><span class="nb">h-means</span>: mini-batch <span class="nb">k-means</span> with a hierarchical warm start and Hartigan assignment</h1>
    <p class="claim">At equal distance computations, <span class="nb">h-means</span> reaches {Math.abs(hi).toFixed(1)}% to {Math.abs(lo).toFixed(1)}% lower <span class="nb">k-means</span> loss than <span class="nb">e-means</span> for 20 or fewer points per cluster, and matches or slightly improves on it for larger clusters.</p>
    <p class="byline">Hélder Monteiro &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/assets/hmeans.pdf">PDF</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="https://github.com/heldernoid/hmeans">Code</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="#cite">Cite</a></p>
    <p class="lede">e-means (Bessa et al., Meta FAIR, 2026) trains k-means on mini-batches with an exponential moving average of cluster statistics and reaches a lower loss than Lloyd's algorithm in a tenth of the passes. h-means keeps the e-means update, laws and schedule, and changes the initialisation and the assignment rule: a hierarchical warm start with loss-proportional allocation of centroids, and Hartigan's exact move rule in place of nearest-centroid assignment. This page is the full account; every number on it comes from the logged runs of one repository.</p>
    <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/playground">Open the playground</a><a class="btn ghost" href="/results">All results</a><a class="btn ghost" href="/assets/hmeans.pdf">Paper (PDF)</a><a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/heldernoid/hmeans">GitHub</a></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p>Three Gaussians plus uniform noise, the first distribution of the e-means playground. All three draw the same points and the same number of centroids (k is printed under each panel) and spend the same number of distance computations per pass. Lloyd and e-means start from the same random rows; since 70% of the points lie in the largest Gaussian, about 70% of their centroids start inside it and stay there, packed so tightly that they read as one disk. h-means allocates centroids in proportion to the loss, so the sparse noise region, where every point is far from its centroid, receives many more of them. The h-means panel looks fuller only because its centroids are spread out where each one is visible. The simulation runs in this tab.</p>
    <div id="sim-hero"></div>
    <p class="small muted">Two dimensions exaggerate the gap (the e-means laws were not fitted there, and faiss switches dead-cluster revival off at d ≤ 2); the measurements below on 96- to 784-dimensional data are the fair comparison.</p>
    <p>With fewer centroids the same thing can be seen cell by cell. Each centroid owns the region of the plane closest to it, its Voronoi cell, and the k-means loss is the sum over cells of the squared distances to the centroid. The cells are tinted by their area. Lloyd and e-means keep most of their centroids inside the big Gaussian, where the cells are tiny, and leave a handful of huge cells over the noise, where every point is far from its centroid. h-means hands the noise many more centroids, so its cells are of more even size and the loss is lower. At this small k the hierarchical warm start costs about one extra pass, which the stat line shows. The checkbox turns the cells on in every simulation on this page and in the playground.</p>
    <div id="sim-voronoi"></div>
  </section>


  <h2 id="gain">The gain, as a function of points per cluster</h2>
  <div class="tiles">
    <div class="tile"><div class="num">{f1(lo)}</div><div class="lab">train loss vs the best e-means at 5 passes, NYTimes k = 32,768 (7 points per cluster), 5 seeds</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="num">{won} / {R.grid.length}</div><div class="lab">configurations (six datasets, Deep-10M included) where h-means is below the better of e-means's two batch laws at equal passes; every seed on each</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="num">3 of 5</div><div class="lab">passes: at 15 points per cluster or fewer, h-means after 3 passes is already below e-means after 5</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="num e">±0.03%</div><div class="lab">train loss vs e-means (quality law) at 240 to 480 points per cluster on SIFT: a tie, the regime where h-means has nothing to add</div></div>
  </div>
  <p>SIFT-128, 980,000 vectors, k from 2,048 to 262,144, three seeds, five passes each. The gain is a function of n/k: a tie above about two hundred points per cluster, increasing below sixty.</p>
  <div class="chart-wrap" id="sweep-chart"><h3>h-means train loss relative to the best e-means, SIFT-128, 5 passes</h3><p class="sub">Negative is h-means better. Bars show ± one seed standard deviation. Hover for the numbers.</p><div class="tip" id="sweep-tip"></div></div>
  <p>At k = 262,144 (four points per cluster) the difference is {f1(sw.vs_best)}. At that scale Lloyd's algorithm is itself competitive with e-means. The summary is that h-means is a better optimiser of the k-means objective when clusters are small, and equivalent to e-means when they are not. Both changes address the same weakness: with few points per cluster, the mini-batch estimate of a cluster's size and position is noisy, and nearest-centroid assignment makes moves that increase the objective.</p>

